from pathlib import Path
import json
from html import escape
from render_research import document,scientific_figure
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
title='Computational and Experimental Investigation of Base-Mediated White Phosphorus Functionalization'
intro='A connectivity-based investigation of P₄ functionalization with PhSSPh and Et₃N, combining candidate-structure generation, Gaussian-based optimisation and experimental monitoring. The reaction target, published mechanistic motif and unverified project pathways are distinguished below.'
figs=[('figure-1-overall.svg',1260,625,900,'Overall reaction','Balanced net reaction with fully drawn P₄, PhSSPh and P(SPh)₃. Et₃N identifies the project additive; this is not a claim of project conversion or yield.'),('figure-2-pathways.svg',1500,1695,1100,'Proposed mechanistic pathways','The generic thiolate/disulfide motif follows Horsley Downie et al., Scheme 2(c). P1–P4 graphs are new, valence-consistent connectivity hypotheses: their existence, geometry and pathway ordering are unestablished. Lower arrows combine attack/cleavage and disulfide capture; they do not depict elementary concerted steps.'),('figure-3-investigation.svg',1420,750,1050,'Computational and experimental investigation','Project workflow based on the supplied research description. No computed minimum, isolated intermediate, NMR assignment, trapping outcome or completed mechanism is implied. The subsequent analytical technique remains unconfirmed.')]
parts=[]
for i,(name,w,h,mw,h2,caption) in enumerate(figs,1):
 f=dict(type='image',src='phosphorus-assets/'+name+'?v=p3',width=w,height=h,min_width=mw,alt=caption,caption=caption)
 parts.append(f'<section class="research-topic" id="figure-{i}"><h2>Figure {i} — {h2}</h2>{scientific_figure(f)}<p class="svg-download"><a href="phosphorus-assets/{name}" download>Download editable SVG ↓</a></p></section>')
rows=[]
D=json.loads((R/'data/phosphorus-connectivity.json').read_text())
for r in D['states']:
 b=lambda es:', '.join('–'.join(e) for e in es) or 'None'
 charge=', '.join('P'+k+': −1' for k,v in r['formal_charges'].items()) or 'All neutral'
 rows.append(f'<tr><th scope="row">{r["id"]}</th><td>{b(r["PP"])}</td><td>{b(r["PS"])}</td><td>{charge}</td><td>{r["status"]}<br>{r["change"]}</td></tr>')
table='<div class="connectivity-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Connectivity audit table. Scroll on small screens."><table><caption>Connectivity audit prepared before diagram rendering</caption><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>P–P bonds</th><th>P–S bonds</th><th>Formal charge</th><th>Assignment and change</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join(rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
body=f'''<header class="detail-heading wrap"><a class="back-link" href="research.html">← Research areas</a><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH 03 / PHOSPHORUS FUNCTIONALIZATION</p><h1>{title}</h1><p>{intro}</p><p class="svg-download"><a href="phosphorus-assets/Research_03_figures.zip" download>Download all three editable SVGs and connectivity audit ↓</a></p></header><div class="phosphorus-content wrap"><nav class="figure-nav" aria-label="Figure navigation"><a href="#figure-1">Overall reaction</a><a href="#figure-2">Candidate pathways</a><a href="#figure-3">Research workflow</a><a href="#connectivity">Connectivity audit</a></nav>{''.join(parts)}<section class="research-topic" id="connectivity"><h2>Connectivity and uncertainty</h2><p>All drawn P–P and P–S bonds are single. Neutral P centres have three bonds and one lone pair; I1 P2 has two bonds, two lone pairs and formal charge −1. Each neutral SPh group has a P–S and an S–C bond. Atom labels track connectivity, not computed coordinates.</p>{table}<p>SPh(1a), etc., are bookkeeping labels, not sulfur provenance assignments. All six initial P₄ edges are symmetry-equivalent. In N1, the four adjacent edges form one class; the opposite P3–P4 edge forms another. A3 and B3 are non-isomorphic star and chain examples, not an exhaustive mechanism.</p><p><a href="data/phosphorus-connectivity.csv" download>Download connectivity table (CSV) ↓</a> · <a href="data/phosphorus-connectivity.md">Read the full audit ↗</a></p></section><section class="research-topic" id="source"><h2>Literature and evidence boundary</h2><p>Horsley Downie et al., <cite>Closing the Loop: Low-Waste Phosphorus Functionalization Enabled by Simple Disulfides</cite>, ChemSusChem 18, e202401895. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/cssc.202401895">DOI: 10.1002/cssc.202401895 ↗</a>. The published motif uses thiolate attack followed by reaction of a phosphorus anion with a disulfide. Scheme 2(a)’s worked example uses KHMDS and a 4-chlorophenyl disulfide; it is not an Et₃N/PhSSPh performance result.</p><p>The initiation step that supplies thiolate under the specified project conditions is left unassigned. No Et₃N–P₄ adduct or pathway-specific transition state is drawn. The labelled candidates satisfy formal valence bookkeeping; this does not establish their chemical existence or stability. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11960583/">Open-access source ↗</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0 ↗</a>. The motif is adapted; atom-labelled graph candidates and workflow artwork are newly drawn.</p></section></div>'''
html=document(title,intro,body,'phosphorus-page').replace('href="nanopore.html" aria-current="page"','href="research.html" aria-current="page"').replace('<script src="research.js"','<link rel="stylesheet" href="phosphorus.css?v=p-1"><script src="research.js"')
html=html.replace('<a href="nanopore.html">Research overview</a>','<a href="research.html">Research areas</a>')
(R/'white-phosphorus.html').write_text(html)
